From e6fa5cb158b56330b41dda6eb299041b3a3d39f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leon Silcott <lnsilcott@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 13:47:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] media: ccs: avoid oversized shifts for tiny floating point
 limits

The floating point limit converter right-shifts a 64-bit mantissa by
-exp without bounding the exponent. Nonzero values with an exponent
of -64 or smaller cause undefined behavior before the final scaling.
For example, raw value 0x044f3000 requests a shift of 119 bits.

Return zero for this underflow range before shifting. Such values are
smaller than one after multiplication by one million. Keep the existing
zero, special-value, saturation and other defined conversion behavior.

The raw-value example was encountered while reviewing the IMX681
limit-data notes in karsies-wq/sp11-imx681-linux at commit
b08f76f40b8d7b715bd4da6aef484f86142cc147. This generic arithmetic fix does
not import sensor limits, change sensor identification or enable cameras.

An exact-function native ARM64 UBSan harness reproduces shifts of 64,
119 and 127 bits before this change. The guarded converter passes 25
explicit cases, 1536 exponent/sign/mantissa combinations and one million
deterministic patterns, preserving all sampled previously defined
results. The CCS object and composite build without warnings.
---
 drivers/media/i2c/ccs/ccs-reg-access.c | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/media/i2c/ccs/ccs-reg-access.c b/drivers/media/i2c/ccs/ccs-reg-access.c
index a0181a5d2f34..9e60ef109aeb 100644
--- a/drivers/media/i2c/ccs/ccs-reg-access.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/ccs/ccs-reg-access.c
@@ -48,6 +48,9 @@ static u32 float_to_u32_mul_1000000(struct i2c_client *client, u32 phloat)
 	 * have 0 in the high bit)
 	 */
 	exp = ((int32_t)phloat >> 23) - 127;
+	/* Such small values round down to zero after scaling. */
+	if (exp <= -64)
+		return 0;
 
 	/* Extract mantissa, add missing '1' bit and it's in MHz */
 	man = ((phloat & 0x7fffff) | 0x800000) * 1000000ULL;
